# Trivy suppressions for the CI image scans (Issues #131, #226).
#
# One file backs all four scans — finance-app, finance-migrate, finance-importer
# and finance-backup — so an entry here silences its CVE in EVERY image, not only
# the one that reported it. That is a deliberate simplification while the file is
# short: it keeps the single reviewable authority CONTRIBUTING.md links to. If
# entries start accumulating, split it into .trivy/<image>.trivyignore and point
# each scan step's `trivyignores:` at its own file, rather than letting one
# image's suppression widen to three that never needed it.
#
# The CI gate blocks on HIGH and CRITICAL findings that have a fix available
# (`ignore-unfixed: true`), so an entry here means "we know, and we have
# decided to ship anyway". That decision needs to be reviewable, so every
# entry must carry:
#
#   1. the CVE / advisory ID
#   2. why it does not apply, or why the fix cannot be taken yet
#   3. an expiry date — Trivy honours `exp:` and the finding returns after it
#
# Format:
#   CVE-YYYY-NNNNN exp:YYYY-MM-DD
#   # <why this is suppressed, and what unblocks removing it>
#
# Prefer rebuilding on a patched base image over adding an entry. If a
# suppression outlives two expiry extensions, that is a signal to change the
# base image rather than extend it a third time.
#
# Two of the four bases are not freely bumpable, which is worth knowing before
# reaching for an entry. postgres:18.6 (finance-backup) must stay version-matched
# to the server, so moving it means moving docker-compose.yml and the workflow
# service containers with it. Both it and python:3.14-slim (finance-importer) are
# rebuilt upstream under the same tag, so for an OS-package finding on either,
# re-running the job after upstream rebuilds is frequently the entire fix — check
# that before suppressing anything.
#
# Populate only from real findings, never pre-emptively.
#
# The entries below were seeded from the first scan of all four images (#226),
# which is the run that turned three previously unscanned images into gated ones.
# Every one of them is a base-image or bundled-tooling finding — none comes from
# code or a dependency this repo declares — and every one is scheduled for review
# on the same date, because the thing that clears most of them is the same event:
# an upstream rebuild of the base image.
#
# A second generation joined them in #291 and has since been pruned in #303,
# which is the first time this file has gone backwards and is worth recording as
# the normal end of an entry's life: the reporter named eight entries as matching
# nothing, and they were deleted rather than left to expire. The widening this
# file's opening note warns about was concrete while they were here — the Debian
# util-linux entries silenced those CVEs on the two Alpine images too, which
# never reported them — and it was concrete again, harmlessly, in the OpenSSL
# entry that sat below until 2026-10-03: that one was reported by all four. Its
# replacements are narrower and say so in their own section, which is where the
# widening question now lives.
#
# NOTHING REMOVES AN ENTRY WHEN THE BASE IS REBUILT. The CVE simply stops being
# reported and the line stays here, inert, until someone deletes it — `exp:` stops
# the suppression working, it does not delete anything.
# scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh runs at the end of the image job and names
# the entries that no longer match a finding in any image; those are the ones to
# remove, along with their comment.

# --- debian 13.7 OS packages (finance-backup, and pcre2 also finance-importer) -
# This section replaces CVE-2026-14456, whose life ran exactly as its own
# comment predicted and so is worth recording. It was suppressed because debian
# had published 3.5.7-1~deb13u2 and no base rebuild carried it yet. The base has
# since been rebuilt, the installed OpenSSL is now that very version, and
# scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh reported the entry as matching nothing in
# any of the four images in the 2026-10-03 run. It was deleted here with its
# comment, which is the normal end of an entry's life this file records for #303.
#
# What replaced it is the same shape one release later: debian 13.7 has published
# -deb13u3 for all three findings below, the postgres:18.6 rebuild has not
# carried it, and the 2026-10-03 run pulled the base fresh and still found
# -deb13u2. Re-running the job cannot clear these today — which is the check the
# header, and CONTRIBUTING.md's step 1, ask for before suppressing an OS-package
# finding on this base. Done the way CONTRIBUTING.md asks and recorded here:
# hub.docker.com/v2/repositories/library/postgres/tags/18.6 reports last_updated
# 2026-09-24, nine days before that run, so the rebuild predates the fix and
# there is no newer base to pull. Re-check the same URL before extending these
# dates; a last_updated after 2026-10-03 means try a re-run first.
#
# NARROWER THAN THE ENTRY THEY REPLACE, BUT NOT AS NARROW AS FIRST RECORDED.
# This paragraph first said all three come from finance-backup alone and that
# the other three scans passed. The second half was wrong, and the correction
# is left visible because the mistake is the kind this file exists to prevent:
# it came from reading only the tail of the job log, where finance-backup is
# the last of the four scans, and inferring that the three scans above it had
# passed. They had not. In the 2026-10-03 17:01 run finance-migrate reported 15
# HIGH and finance-importer 3, and both failed; only finance-app was green.
# Read every scan's summary, not the one next to the error.
#
# What is actually true of these three IDs. CVE-2026-75804 and CVE-2026-84782
# are finance-backup's alone, so for those two the widening the header warns
# about is live and harmless for as long as the other three images do not report
# them. CVE-2026-103111 is not: finance-importer reported it too, against the
# same libpcre2-8-0 and the same fixed version, so that entry covers two images
# by necessity rather than by spillover — which is also why finance-importer's
# debian findings went to zero in the 18:22 run. If an image that does not
# report one of these starts to, that is the signal to split this into
# .trivy/<image>.trivyignore rather than let one image's acceptance speak for
# four.
#
# CVE-2026-14456, for comparison, was reported by all four.

CVE-2026-75804 exp:2026-11-07
# OpenSSL DoS through unenforced QUIC connection flow control. libssl3t64,
# openssl and openssl-provider-legacy 3.5.7-1~deb13u2, fixed in -deb13u3.
#
# WHY IT DOES NOT APPLY. Reaching it requires terminating QUIC. This image is
# `FROM postgres:18.6` plus backup.sh and restore.sh, and it runs pg_dump,
# pg_restore and psql as *clients* against the postgres service over the compose
# network. Postgres speaks its own protocol over TCP and has no QUIC support at
# either end, so nothing here opens a QUIC listener or dials a QUIC peer and the
# flow-control accounting the advisory attacks is never instantiated. This is the
# reasoning CVE-2026-14456 carried, holding for the same reason: the one
# component in the stack that could speak HTTP/3 is caddy, a stock
# caddy:2.11.4-alpine this repo neither builds nor scans.

CVE-2026-84782 exp:2026-11-07
# OpenSSL information disclosure through DTLS handshake retransmission. Same
# three packages and versions as above, fixed in the same -deb13u3.
#
# WHY IT DOES NOT APPLY. DTLS is datagram TLS, and the vulnerable path is a DTLS
# handshake being retransmitted. libpq negotiates TLS over an established TCP
# connection under `sslmode`, never DTLS, and the server it connects to has no
# DTLS listener to talk to. No process in this image builds a DTLS context in
# either direction, so the retransmission path is unreachable rather than merely
# unlikely to be hit.

CVE-2026-103111 exp:2026-11-07
# pcre2 out-of-bounds write through a crafted regular expression. libpcre2-8-0
# 10.46-1~deb13u2, fixed in -deb13u3.
#
# WHY IT DOES NOT APPLY, and why it is reasoned differently from the two above:
# it is not gated behind a network protocol, so "this image is not a server for
# it" would prove nothing. What gates it is who writes the *pattern*. The
# advisory is an out-of-bounds write while compiling a crafted regex, not while
# matching untrusted subject text against a trusted pattern.
#
# This image compiles exactly one regex. backup.sh:58 pipes a psql result into
# `grep -q 1`: the pattern is the literal `1`, written in this repo, and without
# -P grep matches with its own POSIX engine and never calls into pcre2, which it
# links only to offer -P. restore.sh runs no grep at all. Neither script takes a
# pattern from the environment, from a dump, or from a database value. Postgres
# itself does not link pcre2 — its regex engine is the Spencer implementation
# vendored at src/backend/regex — so the server one container over is not a path
# either. libpcre2-8-0 is installed because debian's base packages pull it in for
# that optional grep support, not because anything this image runs reaches it.
#
# THE DATE IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE 2026-11-17 the entries below share, for the
# reason CVE-2026-14456 gave and then vindicated: a fix already published in
# debian and waiting only on a base rebuild is weeks away, not a quarter, so a
# short date forces the re-check instead of parking it. If these three survive to
# 2026-11-07, that is a second consecutive OpenSSL generation whose published fix
# the postgres:18.6 rebuild has skipped, and the header's "change the base image"
# advice stops being theoretical — bounded, as scripts/Dockerfile spells out, by
# having to move docker-compose.yml and the ci.yml and backup-smoke.yml service
# containers in the same step.

# --- python:3.14-slim bundled packages (finance-importer) -------------------
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2026-11-17
# setuptools 70.3.0, bundled in the base image rather than by
# importer/requirements.txt. poll.py never imports it; it is present because the
# image ships pip. Clears on a base rebuild, or could be removed outright if the
# importer image ever drops pip after the install layer.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-11-17
# msgpack 1.1.2, pulled in under the base image's pip tooling, not by
# requirements.txt. Same disposition as setuptools above.
#
# BOTH OF THESE WERE BRIEFLY DELETED IN #291 AND PUT STRAIGHT BACK, which is worth
# recording because the mistake is easy to repeat. A local run of
# scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh reported them stale, and a local
# `ls site-packages` appeared to confirm it. Both were wrong: the machine's cached
# python:3.14-slim was three weeks older than the one CI pulls, and these packages
# live under pip/_vendor/, not at the top level. CI failed on the very next push.
# Trust a staleness verdict from CI, where the image was just built from a freshly
# pulled base — not from a workstation.

# --- npm's own vendored dependencies (finance-migrate) ----------------------
# The migrate stage deliberately keeps npm — it runs `npx drizzle-kit` — where
# the runner stage deletes it precisely because of findings like these. They live
# in /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/ and are fixed only when
# node:24-alpine ships a newer bundled npm; no application-level bump reaches
# them, and `npm update` inside the image would defeat the pinned build.
CVE-2026-14257 exp:2026-11-17
# brace-expansion 5.0.7, fixed in 5.0.8. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-69152 exp:2026-11-17
# brace-expansion 5.0.7, fixed in 5.0.9. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-69192 exp:2026-11-17
# ip-address 10.2.0, fixed in 10.3.1. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-73566 exp:2026-11-17
# tar 7.5.19, fixed in 7.5.21. npm-vendored, as above (#291).
#
# The installed versions above are the point of this block after #303, and they
# are why partial clears are the normal case rather than a curiosity. This
# section held eight entries; node:24-alpine shipped npm 11.19.0, which moved
# tar 7.5.16 → 7.5.19, brace-expansion 5.0.6 → 5.0.7 and undici 6.26.0 → 6.27.0,
# and exactly the four entries whose fix version that reached went dead. These
# four survive because their fix is still ahead of what npm bundles. A vendored
# bump therefore clears *some* of a package's entries and leaves others, so
# re-read the installed version here before assuming a comment is still true —
# these three had drifted a full release behind the image within weeks.

# Not listed here, and excluded at the scanner instead: the Go stdlib CVEs
# reported against drizzle-kit's vendored `esbuild` and the postgres image's
# `gosu`. Both are stripped third-party binaries this repo neither compiles nor
# can patch, so they are `skip-files` entries on their scan steps in ci.yml with
# the reasoning recorded there. Suppressing them by ID would mean a new entry
# every time the Go project publishes a stdlib advisory, forever.
